<P>Let's look individually at these benefits.</P><!-- sidebar begins =
--><!-- don't move sidebars --><!-- sidebar ends -->
<H4>Python interpreter embedded within Apache</H4>
<P>Aside from mod_python, there are two ways of executing Python code by =
Apache.=20
The first is through the CGI model, where a separate process is spawned =
and its=20
standard output is redirected to the browser. The second is by proxying =
request=20
information to another server sitting behind Apache by various methods =
such as=20
FastCGI or proprietary protocols.</P>
<P>CGI, although the most universally supported interface, is by far the =
least=20
efficient. Every CGI request causes a process to be created and the =
Python=20
interpreter loaded and initialized, which results in unnecessary CPU and =
I/O=20
activity.</P>
<P>The second approach of handing the request to a separate application =
server=20
(typically written in Python) is considerably more efficient than CGI =
and is=20
used by a number of popular Python Web development frameworks (Webware, =
for=20
example). It is also very common in the Java world. One shortcoming of =
this=20
approach is the added complexity. You have to configure and run an =
additional=20
server besides Apache, which at least doubles the system administration =
overhead=20
and complicates debugging and troubleshooting. Another shortcoming is =
that it=20
misses out on the amazing scalability and efficiency of Apache, since =
the=20
requests are being serviced only as quickly as the backend server can =
process=20
them.</P>
<P>Mod_python addresses both of these limitations by embedding the =
Python=20
interpreter within Apache. Python code executes directly within the =
Apache=20
server, eliminating any need to spawn external processes or run =
additional=20
servers. At the same time, mod_python takes advantage of Apache's highly =

optimized ability to accept and process incoming requests. The result is =
a=20
platform that can process requests faster than any other currently =
available web=20
application framework for Python.</P>
<P>As a side note, there exists a commonly expressed opinion that, given =
the=20
current speed of hardware, there is no need to devote too much attention =
to=20
optimizing performance from software. Even CGI is fast enough on my=20
multi-gigahertz machine, so why bother with mod_python? Any site can=20
unexpectedly become of interest to very large groups of people, often =
rendering=20
the site inaccessible due to overload (the infamous slashdot effect). =
Because of=20
the continuous growth of the Internet along with ever increasing =
richness of=20
content, even the fastest servers available today are often not able to =
keep up=20
with demand. Therefore any reasonable effort to leverage maximum =
performance of=20
your hardware is well worth it. </P>
<H4>Ability to handle request phases, filters and connections</H4>
<P>The ability to handle request phases, filters, and connections is a =
very=20
Apache-specific feature, but the general concept of phased processing is =
not=20
uncommon. If you have had to program Java Servlets, you will find Apache =
request=20
phases very much like the Servlet interface methods. Each Apache request =
is=20
processed in phases, and modules have an opportunity to register their =
own=20
handler functions for each phase. As an example, =
<CODE>mod_auth_dbm</CODE>=20
registers for the authentication phase, thereby providing the ability to =
use dbm=20
files to store passwords. </P>
<P>Mod_python provides the ability to register for any phase and write =
the=20
processing function in Python. This is a very powerful feature, because =
it opens=20
the door for many innovative and exciting ways to use Apache. For =
example, you=20
can write Python code to do authentication processing or custom logging =
(perhaps=20
sending logs to a database while maintaining real-time statistics).</P>
<P>In addition to phases, mod_python also allows for filters to be =
implemented=20
in Python. Filters receive output from or input to the server and have =
an=20
opportunity to alter it. Filters can also be stacked, so that output =
from one=20
filter is processed by the next filter. A clever filter could =
automatically=20
detect stock ticker symbols and replace them with a link to a site =
providing=20
stock information. </P>
<P>Lastly, mod_python allows you to create connection handlers. A =
connection=20
handler handles the connection from the moment it is received, allowing =
you to=20
bypass Apache's HTTP handling. Connection handlers can be used to =
implement=20
servers for protocols other than HTTP. Although this feature is rarely =
used, it=20
is quite powerful for custom protocol implementation or prototyping =
where=20
performance is important. Many years of work have gone into development =
of=20
Apache's code to handle efficiently large numbers of incoming =
connections. A=20
mod_python connection handler leverages this efficiency as well as =
Apache server=20
facilities such as logging, allowing the developer to focus on the =
protocol=20
handling.</P>
<H4>Interface to Apache API</H4>
<P>The interface to Apache API can be used to retrieve various kinds of =
server=20
information and use internal server facilities. Available server =
information=20
includes typical data available to CGI programs as well as various =
interesting=20
bits such as number of bytes sent, server document root directory, the =
phase=20
being processed, the file handler of the file being requested, and more. =

Additionally, Apache-specific information is available such as the =
configuration=20
tree, the MPM being used, and MPM parameters such as the maximum number =
of=20
processes and threads.</P>
<P>Aside from getting information, the exposed portion of API can be =
used to do=20
take some very powerful actions such as dynamically register additional=20
mod_python handlers, create internal redirects, and register cleanups to =
be=20
executed after the request is finished or before the server is shut =
down. </P>
<H3>Web Development Toolset</H3>
<P>Early versions of mod_python provided few tools for web development. =
This=20
made it difficult for developers who were not interested in delving into =
Apache=20
internals, yet who wanted an efficient and scalable platform for =
developing=20
applications in Python. Although mod_python included the Publisher =
handler,=20
which implements a clever way of mapping URI paths to functions and =
objects=20
within Python modules (originally inspired by Zope's Zpublisher, back =
then known=20
as Bobo), it did not provide a native way of dealing with cookies and =
sessions.=20
The latter proved to be a particularly complex issue due to Apache's=20
multi-process architecture, which makes sharing data and locking between =

processes rather challenging for an average programmer.</P>
<P>Luckily, this has changed in the upcoming (as of the time of writing) =
version=20
3.1. This release introduces native handling of cookies, including =
support for=20
cryptographic signing of cookie data (using HMAC), as well as the =
ability to=20
marshal (serialize) simple objects into cookie values. There is support =
for=20
session management with fairly thorough random session id generation =
logic and=20
the ability to take advantage of signed cookies. Sessions can be stored =
in=20
either a dbm file or directly in memory depending on whether Apache runs =
in=20
multi-process or threaded mode. Sessions support session locks using =
Apache's=20
internal global mutex interface to provide mutual exclusion across all =
processes=20
and threads. The <CODE>Session</CODE> class is extensible, so that it is =
easy to=20
implement custom session objects which use alternative persistent =
storage, such=20
as a relational database.</P>
<P>Last, but not the least, version 3.1 introduces mod_python's own=20
implementation of PSP (Python Server Pages). This is a framework that =
allows=20
embedding Python code within HTML similar to the way it is done in PHP, =
JSP, or=20
ASP. The core parser implementation for mod_python PSP was initially =
written and=20
graciously contributed to mod_python by Sterling Hughes, a core PHP =
developer.=20
The PSP parser is generated using <CODE>flex </CODE>, one of the fastest =

scanning and parsing tools in existence. It also integrates nicely with =
other=20
tools provided by mod_python such as session handling, altogether =
resulting in=20
one of the fastest Server Page implementations available.</P>
<H3>Mod_python Constraints</H3>
<P>The only real constraint of mod_python is that it is Apache-specific. =

Mod_python applications are not easily portable to other web server =
platforms.=20
In my opinion this is not a concern, because Apache is already supported =
by a=20
larger number of operating systems than any other web server and is by =
far the=20
most popular web server in the world, and still gaining market share. By =

committing to Apache as an integral part of your application, you gain =
an=20
amazing amount of performance and versatility at the expense of not =
having the=20
ability to use a (less capable) web server down the road. I think this =
is a=20
no-brainer.</P>
<H3>Mod_python is an ASF project</H3>
<P>Finally, I'd like to mention that shortly before release of version =
3.0,=20
mod_python was adopted by Apache Software Foundation and became an =
official=20
subproject of the Apache HTTP Server project. This gave mod_python a =
wider=20
recognition and the necessary foundation of sound development practices =
and peer=20
review of a very talented team of developers. Mod_python's popularity is =

steadily growing as more and more developers recognize it a sound and =
stable=20
platform for Web application development. As an example, Red Hat 9 now =
has=20
mod_python enabled by default.</P>
<H3>Conclusion</H3>
<P>Mod_python is a fast, versatile and scalable way to develop Web =
applications=20
in Python. If you're convinced, give it a try.</P>
<P><I><A href=3D"http://onlamp.com/pub/au/1304">Gregory Trubetskoy</A> =
is the lead=20
developer of mod_python and a member of the Apache Software Foundation. =
</I></P>
